## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical understanding of tokenization-to-attention flow in a real task like headline classification. It connects a toy numeric example to concepts used in production.

## Key Concepts
- Self-attention with Q, K, V
- Scaling by sqrt(dk) and softmax
- How token representations influence weights and output

## Code Example
```javascript
function attention(q, K, V) {
  // q: 1xd, K: nxd, V: nxd
  const dk = q.length;
  const scores = K.map(k => dot(q, k) / Math.sqrt(dk));
  const weights = softmax(scores);
  const out = [0,0].map((_,i) => weights.reduce((acc, w, idx) => acc + w * V[idx][i], 0));
  return {weights, out};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to multi-head attention and what changes in compute cost?
- How do embedding size and padding affect numerical stability and softmax behavior?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical understanding of retrieval-augmented NLP basics using a concrete tech stack (MongoDB Atlas Vector Search) and a beginner-friendly workflow.

## Key Concepts
- Chunking strategy for long docs
- Embedding models and cosine similarity
- Vector database indexing and search
- Simple result assembly and error handling

## Code Example
```javascript
// Example chunking
function chunkText(text, maxChars=512){
  const words = text.split(/\\s+/);
  let chunk = '', chunks = [];
  for (const w of words){
    if ((chunk + ' ' + w).length > maxChars){
      chunks.push(chunk.trim()); chunk = w;
    } else {
      chunk += ' ' + w;
    }
  }
  if (chunk) chunks.push(chunk.trim());
  return chunks;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle overlapping chunks to preserve context?
- How would you validate results when the top-k chunks disagree?

## Why This Is Asked
